  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Even less t-storms today with increasing chances \nfor the new workweek. Turning hotter with high humidity continuing. \n\nNot much of a change in the overall synoptic and mesoscale pattern \nas we continue in this typical August summertime regime. It's a \nrinse and repeat forecast of hot temperatures, high humidity, and \ndiurnal thunderstorms chances. \n\nStorm coverage will be at it's lowest today and potentially \nlingering into Monday as we sit between shortwave impulses. The \nstrengthening subtropical ridge offshore combined with west to \nsouthwest flow aloft should aid in the suppression of convection \ntoday outside of the usual terrain and bay breeze circulations. \nCurrent 00z guidance keeps things fairly isolated over the terrain \nand in locations mainly along and south of I-64. Any thunderstorms \nthat pop up will be short-lived with gusty winds, locally heavy \nrainfall, and frequent lightning as the primary concerns. \n\nAs for Monday, the chances for thunderstorms have increased given \nanother piece of shortwave energy ejecting across the region. \nCurrent 00z guidance suggests scattered to numerous thunderstorms \nacross the region with a focus east of the Alleghenies Monday \nafternoon and evening. SPC currently has most of the region in a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) for severe weather with a Slight \nRisk (Level 2 out of 5) grazing northwest Garrett Co. MD. Machine \nguidance is also high on the severe threat with WxNext2 \nprobabilities of 30-60 percent and CSU probabilities of 15 to 30 \npercent across the area during the Monday into Tuesday timeframe. \nEven with that said, coverage and timing remain the two big question \nmarks similar to what we saw on Saturday across the region. With a \nhot and humid airmass in place and marginal shear this will be \nsomething to watch in the near term period. \n\nAdditional shortwave disturbances look to pass through Tuesday into \nWednesday and again Wednesday into Thursday. Uncertainty remains in \nregards to timing and coverage which will dictate the risk for \nsevere weather. Current machine learning guidance and day 4-8 \noutlooks from SPC mention at least a chance for severe weather each \nday mainly during the afternoon and evening hours. Ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance put us in an MCS setup which will make it \neven harder to time out severe weather, especially with complexes of \nstorms moving around the top of the ridge. Once again something that \nwill continue to monitor in the coming days ahead. \n\nOutside of he storm chances expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s with heat indices east of the Blue Ridge up around 100 degrees. \nThe hottest days look to be today and Monday given the strengthened \nsubtropical ridge nearby. Highs will push into the mid 90s with heat \nindices closer to 105 degrees east of the Blue Ridge/along the urban \ncorridor Monday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front could arrive late next week bringing \nrelief from the heat and humidity into the upcoming weekend. \n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Saturday as a \nmore potent frontal system tracks toward the region. Ahead of that \nfront will be several fast moving shortwave disturbances which will \nhelp aid in shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiod. Storm coverage is a bit uncertain Thursday into late week as \nthe front tries to push through. Speaking of the front, details \nstill remain muddled on it's passage. Most 00z ensemble guidance \nsuggests a frontal passage late Thursday into Friday while others \nhave a front Friday into Saturday. Other deterministic members try \nto stall the front to the south Friday into Saturday while bringing \nit back north through the end of the weekend into early next week.\n\nWith that said, there is even more uncertainty in regards to severe \nweather Thursday through Saturday based upon cloud cover and the \nplacement of the front. Of course any decent frontal passage we'll \nhave to be monitor given machine probabilities remaining between 5-\n15 percent for severe weather. Temperatures will cool from the 90s \ninto the low to mid 80s late week. Overnight lows will fall back \ninto the low to mid 60s with 50s over the mountains.",
